  "description": "The current internal layout consists of compartmentalised rooms, which restrict spatial flow and limit interaction among family members. This proposal seeks to enhance spatial connectivity by removing an internal wall, thereby creating a cohesive open plan lounge, kitchen, and dining area. This design intervention aims to foster a greater sense of interaction and engagement within the household, particularly enabling visual and physical connectivity between the kitchen and living space to facilitate multitasking, such as cooking while supervising a child.To optimise natural daylight penetration and establish a stronger connection between the interior and exterior spaces, large Crittall style aluminum framed doors and windows are proposed along the rear facade. Additionally, the existing garage, presently unutilised, will be repurposed into a habitable utility room, directly linked to the proposed kitchen layout. This conversion will improve functional efficiency while also offering additional storage space for the occupants. The existing garage door will be retained, and the front elevation fenestration will be updated by replacing the existing windows with black aluminium frames. The style of the windows will remain unchanged, aligning with the aesthetic of neighbouring properties, many of which have also adopted black framed windows.",
